#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15719b6-0ce4-5fb8-80be-abcaf9344a10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02cd50ee-7dfb-5329-b414-a7e29e6d91af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269294} {:answer/id #uuid "0e3ed43b-9eec-5604-965c-4bda6251204f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269393} {:answer/id #uuid "71430654-a5e1-51d5-b25d-4d132a09c4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270275} {:answer/id #uuid "8b96600f-4cfc-560d-a286-0ee5f9d41773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270478} {:answer/id #uuid "d5cb0b79-a29d-51af-b029-65c79862a96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5cb0b79-a29d-51af-b029-65c79862a96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271144} :answer-field/id #uuid "61209408-2d37-576f-b6f0-97778128a9a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "915cd440-9e70-527f-ae9d-6fd020966a43" :question/math-academy-id "q-140529" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 3}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $b=2,$ $c=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}=-4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e43d27-ffaf-58ea-bb6e-664b70770003" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Span of Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "048ef5c8-0a10-53a5-bbcf-1d721a524144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414721} {:answer/id #uuid "05da9795-05f5-5040-9da5-00cee056fe08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414722} {:answer/id #uuid "1326ecd9-681d-55f9-841c-c7013851f621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414723} {:answer/id #uuid "292aa1fd-4f1b-5b3f-b502-e340aa086aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414726} {:answer/id #uuid "502fbad5-6108-585e-9a6e-c3c7e917352d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1326ecd9-681d-55f9-841c-c7013851f621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414723} :answer-field/id #uuid "219cb7dd-0360-5f51-af53-0c31b7c77c47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7bb7823-c38b-5250-a2f7-3b306162dae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-40736" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}},\\,B={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{17}{6} \\\\ \\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{17}{6} \\\\ \\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=\\frac{17}{6},$ $b=\\frac{11}{6},$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(\\frac{17}{6})}{(\\frac{11}{6})}=\\frac{17}{11}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccdefb8e-1bb5-52e9-812d-d2a5bffff731" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthonormal Basis of a Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18614843-53b5-5af0-b228-36c44c24c90d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324183} {:answer/id #uuid "1d219cdb-66db-5669-87c6-a207fcc3a1f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324216} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac27b02-75a3-57e4-bb6d-c8d6b1bc2cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324577} {:answer/id #uuid "5fca2e57-483c-5d7c-8d41-b3a55edeffbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324771} {:answer/id #uuid "c56558d4-a506-50db-8943-d35da70e291a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d219cdb-66db-5669-87c6-a207fcc3a1f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324216} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3be7ad6-26c9-556b-b096-8959f855c4f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2ffd5b6e-c9be-5714-a520-bbfc949845de" :question/math-academy-id "q-40743" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B={{u}_{1},{u}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}$ is an orthonormal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ $c=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e43d27-ffaf-58ea-bb6e-664b70770003" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Span of Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96e893c4-4997-5c8e-b8d7-c8971f6c56a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132056} {:answer/id #uuid "a96063c6-ce04-5df0-8bfe-418753e9924d" :answ